public class ErpCustomerSync {

    /**
     * Maximum number of accounts per async transaction.
     * Salesforce limits callouts to 100 per transaction, even inside future methods.
     */
    private static final Integer MAX_ACCOUNTS_PER_FUTURE = 100;

    /**
     * Entry point called from the Account trigger.
     * Callouts are not supported from trigger context, so we dispatch to
     * asynchronous future methods. Each account is still processed with its
     * own individual callout so the ERP can trace every account separately.
     */
    public static void syncCustomers(List<Account> accounts) {
        if (accounts == null || accounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Id> accountIds = new List<Id>();
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            accountIds.add(acc.Id);
        }

        // Chunk into groups of 100 to stay within the per-transaction callout limit.
        Integer chunkSize = MAX_ACCOUNTS_PER_FUTURE;
        for (Integer i = 0; i < accountIds.size(); i += chunkSize) {
            List<Id> chunk = new List<Id>();
            for (Integer j = i; j < Math.min(i + chunkSize, accountIds.size()); j++) {
                chunk.add(accountIds[j]);
            }
            syncCustomersAsync(chunk);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Asynchronous handler that performs the actual ERP callouts.
     * Annotated with callout = true because HTTP callouts are disallowed
     * from plain future methods.
     */
    @future(callout = true)
    public static void syncCustomersAsync(List<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Query the accounts to ensure we have the latest data.
        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, Name, BillingCountry
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :accountIds
        ];

        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            syncCustomer(acc);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Makes a single callout to the ERP for one account.
     * The body is a JSON array with one element, as specified by the ERP API.
     */
    private static void syncCustomer(Account acc) {
        if (acc == null) {
            return;
        }

        // Treat blank BillingCountry as null per the ERP API specification.
        String country = String.isBlank(acc.BillingCountry) ? null : acc.BillingCountry;

        CustomerPayload payload = new CustomerPayload(
            String.valueOf(acc.Id), // 18-character Salesforce Id
            acc.Name,
            country
        );

        List<CustomerPayload> payloadList = new List<CustomerPayload> { payload };

        Http http = new Http();
        HttpRequest request = new HttpRequest();
        request.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
        request.setMethod('POST');
        request.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        request.setBody(JSON.serialize(payloadList));

        HttpResponse response = http.send(request);

        // Error handling and retries are out of scope for this ticket.
        // The ERP answers 202 Accepted on success.
    }

    /**
     * Inner class used to serialize the customer payload.
     */
    public class CustomerPayload {
        public String salesforceId;
        public String name;
        public String country;

        public CustomerPayload(String salesforceId, String name, String country) {
            this.salesforceId = salesforceId;
            this.name = name;
            this.country = country;
        }
    }
}
```

**Why I deviated from "make the callout right from the trigger":** Salesforce does not permit HTTP callouts (`Http.send`) from trigger execution context. The only supported patterns are asynchronous (`@future(callout = true)`, Queueable, or Platform Events). Therefore the trigger enqueues the work and the future method performs the callouts. Each account still results in exactly one separate HTTP request, preserving the per-account traceability the ERP team requires. Batches larger than 100 are chunked across multiple future invocations to remain within the 100-callout-per-transaction governor limit.

