public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> results = new List<Id>();
        for (Integer i = 0; i < leads.size(); i++) {
            results.add(null);
        }

        for (Integer i = 0; i < leads.size(); i++) {
            Lead lead = leads[i];
            String leadEmail = lead.Email == null ? '' : lead.Email.trim().toLowerCase();

            if (leadEmail.isBlank()) {
                continue;
            }

            for (Contact contact : contacts) {
                String contactEmail = contact.Email == null ? '' : contact.Email.trim().toLowerCase();

                if (contactEmail.isBlank()) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (contactEmail.equals(leadEmail)) {
                    results[i] = contact.Id;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return results;
    }
}
```

The only change was replacing `isEmpty()` with `isBlank()` on both `leadEmail` and `contactEmail`. Everything else stays the same: the nested loop preserves contacts order (first match wins), trims and lowercases for case-insensitive matching, and blank emails never match.

A brief note: the brief asks for an org-wide contact map, but on an org with ~2.3 M contacts that query would exceed the 50 k SOQL row limit and the heap limit. The bulk-safe equivalent is one selective query for contacts belonging to the accounts whose `BillingCountry` actually changed. No queries run in loops, and DML is chunked at 200.

